15053	{"artifactId":"LLD-7fefc3fc5c51793e-s1","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-7fefc3fc5c51793e-s1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T07:05:32.151Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every element of the mapping in SPEC-3d2161bcef24cecf (bars, bubbles, tool rows, the five markers, diff lines, approval card and buttons, decided badge, selection card, input, selects, pill, attachment chips, progress, markdown, focus, monospace font) is designed onto a theme variable.","evidence":"The layer at vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts:599-629 carries each SPEC variable (for example `--bg-inset:var(--vscode-editorWidget-background)`, `--warn-bg:var(--vscode-inputValidation-warningBackground)`, `--pass:var(--vscode-testing-iconPassed)`, `--progress:var(--vscode-progressBar-background)`, `--font` and `--sans` both `var(--vscode-editor-font-family)`). The rules at 634-769 and render-registry.ts:189-256 use them as the design's section 3 lists. I compared the SPEC `intent` text element by element and found none without a name. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/render-registry.ts, .insrc/artifacts/SPEC-3d2161bcef24cecf.json]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The two departures from the approved SPEC (one color-mix for the assistant ground, and the assistant bubble taking chat.requestBorder instead of editorWidget.border) rest on a user decision of 2026-10-13 that overrides the SPEC.","evidence":"The SPEC still lists as a non-goal \"Fixed or fallback hex/rgb colours, and color-mix derived tints\" and maps \"assistant message editor.background with editorWidget.border\"; its `decisions` array is empty. The override is asserted only by the design itself, by a code comment at chat-panel.ts:606-608 (\"The one derived colour, asked for by the user on 2026-10-13\") and by commit subjects (f2289cf0, 5744d9e3, 157634e5 \"revise the dev chat theme design with the user's decisions made during the build\"). No approved artifact I could read records the user's decision itself. [files: .insrc/artifacts/SPEC-3d2161bcef24cecf.json, vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts]","action":"Confirm with the user that both departures are theirs, and record them where the SPEC is read (amend the SPEC's non-goal and assistant-message mapping, or add the decisions to it) so the design no longer contradicts its upstream.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The four cited code facts are true at the cited places: the content security policy string, RENDER_REGISTRY_STYLE, DOCS_REVIEW_MARKDOWN_TOKENS and renderTerminalStyle.","evidence":"chat-panel.ts:585 reads ``const csp = `default-src 'none'; img-src data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'nonce-${nonce}';`;``; render-registry.ts:189 `export const RENDER_REGISTRY_STYLE =`; markdown-style.ts:121 `export const DOCS_REVIEW_MARKDOWN_TOKENS: MarkdownStyleTokens = {`; design-tokens.ts:174 `export function renderTerminalStyle(theme: TerminalTheme = terminalTheme): string {`. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/render-registry.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/markdown-style.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/design-tokens.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The shared token sheet supplies the marker glyphs to the chat and the shell only re-tones them.","evidence":"design-tokens.ts:220-224 set the glyph, e.g. `.insrc-term__marker--pending::before { content: var(--it-mk-pending); color: var(--it-accent); }`; the shell at chat-panel.ts:651-655 only overrides the colour, e.g. `.insrc-term__marker--pending::before{color:var(--magenta)!important;margin-right:.55em;}`. The shell embeds the sheet first (`${style}${layoutStyle}` at line 973). [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/design-tokens.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"MarkdownStyleTokens has no field for inline code's own ground, the block quote ground and border, the rule's colour or a separate link colour, so the shell must add them after the shared rules; renderMarkdownStyle is reused unchanged.","evidence":"markdown-style.ts:88-92: inline code and `pre` both use `${t.codeBg}`; `.insrc-md a{color:${t.accent}` shares `accent` with inline code; `hr` uses `${t.border}`; the block quote has only `border-left:2px solid ${t.borderLit}` and no ground. CHAT_MARKDOWN_TOKENS (100-112) has `accent: 'var(--pre-fg)'`, `codeBg: 'var(--code-bg)'`, `fontSans: 'var(--sans)'` as designed. The shell adds the four overrides at chat-panel.ts:761-763, after `renderMarkdownStyle(CHAT_MARKDOWN_TOKENS)` at 758. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/markdown-style.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The decided badge can take its passed and failed colours from the selectors `.insrc-permoutcome__badge.insrc-approval__btn--approve` and `--deny` without changing the renderer's class names.","evidence":"The renderer already sets both classes on the badge: render-registry.ts:531 `className='insrc-permoutcome__badge insrc-approval__btn--'+(approved?'approve':'deny');`. The two doubled selectors are at 236-237 and come after the button rules at 222-223, so they win. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/render-registry.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The shell's `:root` layer is the one place the chat's colours are chosen: every colour the chat shows comes from a name in that layer, with no literal fallback.","evidence":"The chat document also embeds the shared sheet, and two of its rules out-rank or supplement the shell. The body is `<body class=\"insrc-term\">` (chat-panel.ts:980), and design-tokens.ts:183-203 gives `.insrc-term { ... font-family: var(--it-font); font-size: var(--it-size); line-height: var(--it-line); background: var(--it-bg); color: var(--it-fg); }`. A class selector beats the shell's element rule `body{...background:var(--bg);color:var(--fg);font-family:var(--font);...}` (line 634), so the body's ground, text colour and font are the sheet's `--it-*` values, e.g. `--it-bg: var(--vscode-editor-background, #0b0e14)`, each with a fixed literal fallback. The diff container carries `.insrc-term-diff`, which design-tokens.ts:207 paints with `background: var(--it-bg); color: var(--it-fg)`; the shell's own `.insrc-term-diff` rule (line 727) sets only border, radius, margin and overflow. The code comment at chat-panel.ts:974-979 describes this same class-over-`body` mechanism. In VS Code the result is the same theme variables, so nothing looks wrong, but the design's inventory misses it, and the test `has('body', 'background:var(--bg)', ...)` checks that the declaration is present, not that it wins. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/design-tokens.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__/chat-theme.test.ts]","action":"Either state the exception in the design (the body and the diff container are painted by the shared sheet's `.insrc-term` and `.insrc-term-diff` rules, which resolve to the same theme variables), or have the shell out-rank them (for example a `body.insrc-term` rule and a ground and colour on the shell's `.insrc-term-diff` rule) and add a test that the winning declaration is a name.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The three listed call sites are the complete set: RENDER_REGISTRY_STYLE and CHAT_MARKDOWN_TOKENS are used only by the chat shell, and no colour is set from script or inline markup.","evidence":"A search of vscode-plugin finds RENDER_REGISTRY_STYLE used outside tests only at chat-panel.ts:736 and CHAT_MARKDOWN_TOKENS only at chat-panel.ts:758. A search for `.style.`, `style=`, `cssText`, hex and rgb values finds nothing in render-registry.ts, and in chat-panel.ts only the attach button's SVG with `stroke=\"currentColor\"` (line 1016) and the mask data URI (line 725). [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/render-registry.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.3","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Wrapping the history select in `.histchip` leaves the select's id, class, options and script wiring intact, and the two chat-panel.test.ts tests that pinned the old rule assert the same intent on the wrapper.","evidence":"chat-panel.ts:988 `<span class=\"histchip\"><select id=\"insrc-history\" class=\"segsel ${histCls}\" aria-label=\"session\"><option value=\"\">new…</option></select></span>`; the script still binds by id at line 858 (`document.getElementById('insrc-history')`). `.histchip` at 721 carries `max-width:36ch;padding:3px 2px 3px 22px;...border:1px solid var(--dropdown-border);border-radius:6px`; `::before` at 724 has `left:6px;...width:12px`. chat-panel.test.ts:1119-1135 and 1453-1471 assert the 36ch cap, border, radius, padding, the mask and one wrapper. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__/chat-panel.test.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"No stored shape changes: a restored chat is drawn from class names alone, so older stored rows take theme colours with no migration.","evidence":"The row renderer builds elements through `className`/`textContent` (render-registry.ts:278-292 and 531) and searches for inline styles or colour literals in render-registry.ts found none. The design changes only CSS strings and one wrapper span in the shell markup; no message, protocol or session-store file is among its change sites. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/render-registry.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The docs-review panel uses none of the three style sources this design changes, so its styles cannot be affected.","evidence":"docs-review-panel.ts:14-19 imports `renderTerminalStyle, surfaceClass, terminalTheme` from design-tokens.js, `attr, type ChatPanelChannel, type ChatPanelLogger` from chat-panel.js and `renderMarkdownStyle, DOCS_REVIEW_MARKDOWN_TOKENS` from markdown-style.js; it builds its style at line 1489 from `renderMarkdownStyle(DOCS_REVIEW_MARKDOWN_TOKENS)`. DOCS_REVIEW_MARKDOWN_TOKENS (markdown-style.ts:121-135) still holds only `--it-*` names. The design is standalone, so there is no HLD contract to check. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-review-panel.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/markdown-style.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each failure the design says a test catches is caught by a test: an undefined name, an unused name, a fixed colour or second color-mix or literal fallback, and a theme variable outside the mapping (both directions).","evidence":"chat-theme.test.ts:147-163 collects every `var(--name)` in the shell style, RENDER_REGISTRY_STYLE and the chat's markdown rules, fails on a missing name, and fails when a layer name is never used. Lines 103-123 require the `--assistant-bg:color-mix(...)` declaration exactly once, allow one `color-mix(`, and reject `rgb(`, `rgba(`, `hsl(`, `radial-gradient(` and any `var(--x, literal)`. Lines 175-176 compare the referenced `--vscode-*` set with APPROVED_THEME_VARIABLES in both directions; lines 180-189 pin each optional fallback chain. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__/chat-theme.test.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The facts about VS Code and the browser the error handling rests on are true: every `--vscode-*` name in the mapping exists, a high-contrast light body also carries the plain `vscode-high-contrast` class, and a CSS mask data URI is governed by `img-src`.","evidence":"These are properties of VS Code and the browser, not of this repository; nothing in the repo (no VS Code source, no theme dump) lets me confirm them. The tests only compare the names with the design's own list, so a name spelt as designed that VS Code does not define would pass. The design says the same: \"can only be seen in the panel\". [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/chat-panel.ts]","action":"Keep the smoke test in VS Code as the proof, including one high-contrast light theme to confirm the colour-scheme rule order.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every test the acceptance mapping names for ac1, ac2 and ac3 exists under that exact title, with the before-change fixture it needs.","evidence":"chat-theme.test.ts declares seven tests at lines 96, 147, 165, 212, 295, 315 and 349 whose titles match the design's seven unit and integration subjects word for word. chat-theme-before.json exists and holds `selectors`, `ids`, `styleBlocks`, `sharedSheetSha256` and `docsReviewMarkdownSha256`, read at lines 316 and 350. The eighth subject (the panel in real themes) is a manual smoke test by design. The SPEC itself lists no acceptance criteria, so I could check the mapping only against the design's own ac1-ac3. [files: vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__/chat-theme.test.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__/chat-theme-before.json]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":1,"low":13,"unverified":2},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-13T07:05:32.151Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
